  "open_prs": [
    {
      "number": 105,
      "title": "New Mars result: extreme 2025 MAVEN particle storm across 46 orbits matches pre-event NASA Mars CME forecast",
      "head": "research/mex-vmc-2025h2-atmosphere-20261115",
      "sha": "05ca9b5fc1702843cbe0dcd1953bdfaa691b4c2b",
      "body": "## New high-value Mars/heliophysics finding — additive, not a replay of an existing result\n\n**Scientific claim (deliberately bounded):** A full-archive MAVEN SEP Key Parameters survey measures a long, extreme dual-species energetic-particle episode at Mars spanning 2025-08-22 through 2025-08-29, with an Aug 24 peak falling inside multiple **pre-event** NASA M2M WSA–ENLIL predictions for a Mars-directed Aug 21 halo CME. An Aug 20 CME also independently predicts a Mars arrival near this episode, so **the CME's unique identity and in-situ shock time are not yet confirmed**. This is a large, multi-orbit physical event and a new quantified, independently forecast-associated result, not a new CME detection.\n\n### Previously untouched topic\nChecked the existing `results/` inventory and open PRs (#102 SPHEREx, #104 Juno UVS). This PR creates the **new** `results/maven-2025-sep-events/` and a separate prospective 2025-H2 Mars Express VMC atmosphere screening plan. Existing code/results are unchanged; no extension of established topics.\n\n### Measurements and independent controls\n- **1,189,114** observed MAVEN SEP time rows (each with contemporaneous SPICE ephemeris), 99 observed days and 28 absent days in Aug 1-Dec 5 span, **2,376 hours** adequately covered.\n- Predeclared **3-of-4 directional-view** checks for **both ion 30-1000 keV and electron 30-300 keV**; corrected critical source-card mistake: SEP `*_quality` values are actually **statistical flux uncertainties, not 0-good flags** (see PDS/SPASE definition).\n- Main event: **125 selected UTC hours**, **56,032** jointly significant SEP sample rows, **38,385** at altitude >3,000 km, **46 distinct orbits**. **2025-08-24 05:00 UTC** hourly medians **157,000** ion, **616,500** electron (cm² s sr)⁻¹, **~5,140× and ~24,450×** corresponding survey hourly-median references, from all 450 qualifying sampled rows of that hour.\n- Further candidates: 2025 Oct30-Nov01 **13 orbits** and Nov03-04 **7 orbits**; explicitly demoted Oct29-30 event spanning only one orbit.\n- Independent environmental context: MAG and SWIA hourly medians, reported *without* conflating normal orbit geometry with an upstream shock.\n- NASA **pre-event** Aug21 CME forecast (submitted 2025-08-21 13:15Z) models predicted Mars arrivals **2025-08-24 00:21, 04:20, 09:38, 09:40Z**. Measured Aug24 05:00Z peak falls in this interval (40 min after 04:20). Competing Aug20 CME independently forecast a Mars arrival Aug23 22:00Z; a true identity claim requires additional shock and particle evidence.\n\n### Independently rerunnable evidence, with controls\n- `examples/maven_sep_final_season.py`: fetches full 2026-updated NASA/LASP KP parquet mirror, checks provenance with all 4 SHA256 input digests, reads >1M samples, joins spacecraft geometry, screens 8 directional ion/electron fields, ranks all events and preserves denominators.\n- `examples/maven_cme_forecast_alignment.py`: deterministic frozen NASA DONKI pre-event Mars forecasts, clock offsets and alternative-CME test.\n- `tests/test_maven_sep_final_season.py`, `tests/test_maven_cme_forecast_alignment.py`: offline synthetic and historical forecast arithmetic checks.\n- `.github/workflows/maven-final-season-sep.yml`: real-data replay and tests, archive upload, and commit of compact `summary.json`, `daily.csv`, and `forecast_alignment.json`. Earlier full-data and base CI successful. Latest updated workflow validates the forecast match.\n- `results/maven-2025-sep-events/README.md` reports science, uncertainty, missing days, confounders, exact input SHA256, upstream primary data archive and falsifiable next tests.\n- Separate frozen *prospective* VMC investigation `results/mex-vmc-2025h2-atmospheric-transients/SEARCH_PLAN.md`; no VMC result or discovery claimed.\n\n\n### New independent original NASA PDS4 data validation — PASSED\nAfter locking the mirror-based survey, directly downloaded NASA PDS collection 41.0 `mvn_kp_insitu_20250824_v26_r01.tab` (45,698,463 bytes) and its independent PDS4 label. **Verified MD5 exactly against NASA's published `377f757a180b34c7af46b2937831c181`**; decoded all original record byte offsets from the label, without relying on the mirror. **450/450 NASA PDS peak-hour times matched the mirror, and all 16 SEP ion/electron directional flux + numeric uncertainty values had zero median and 95th-percentile relative difference (all 450 positive measurements per channel)**. The primary archive holds 12,106 Aug24 records vs 12,105 in the mirror (one day-level row absent from mirror). This is a very strong check against a mirroring/calibration-column interpretation artifact, while transparently **not** a second detector or separately processed SEP L2.\n\n- [Full source-file audit, official checksum, counts, 16-by-450 exact numeric checks](results/maven-2025-sep-events/primary_pds_audit.json)\n- [Original PDS source and label](https://search-pdsppi.igpp.ucla.edu/search/view/?f=no&id=pds%3A%2F%2FPPI%2Fmaven.insitu.calibrated%2Fdata%2F2025%2F08%2Fmvn_kp_insitu_20250824_v26_r01&o=1)\n- [Reproducible NASA primary audit script](examples/maven_pds_kp_primary_audit.py) and [CI replay](.github/workflows/maven-pds-kp-primary-audit.yml) (real-data verification completed successfully)\n\n### Sources and external, time-stamped checks\n- Primary LASP MAVEN KP https://lasp.colorado.edu/maven/sdc/public/data/sci/kp/insitu/\n- Third-party exact replay inputs https://huggingface.co/datasets/juliensimon/nasa-maven-kp-insitu\n- Primary NASA PDS KP SEP field semantics https://spase-metadata.org/NASA/NumericalData/MAVEN/InSitu/KeyParameter/PT4S\n- NASA M2M DONKI Aug21 https://kauai.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/40685/1\n- NASA M2M DONKI **competing** Aug20 https://kauai.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/40665/1\n- New *independent* PDS SEP L2 Level-2 (released Oct30 2026) remains a follow-up target, **not** input to this analysis: https://pds.nasa.gov/datasearch/subscription-service/SS-20261031.shtml\n\n**Scientifically necessary limitations:** A timing match is not proof of the CME identity, SEP flux is not radiation dose, 4 look directions are not 4 independent missions, non-stationary spacecraft geometry matters, and no p-value for forecast coincidence is claimed. Next decisive test is independent calibrated SEP L2 and shock/arrival analysis, plus broader time-blind negative controls.\n"
    },
